-| 3.1 | Studies on multiple-response scoring generally find **partial-credit rules perform better psychometrically than all-or-nothing**, including Ripkey's rule and balanced rules; the differences are sometimes small. The app's rule (credit for correctly *unselected* options too) is a form of "balanced" scoring but gives a non-zero score to a blank answer — compare it with other rules. See [Psychometrika framework](https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/psychometrika/article/psychometric-model-framework-for-multiple-response-items/10D6DCD514D67281E4695FFC00C88B6A) and the 2021 EPM paper "Evaluating Different Scoring Methods for Multiple Response Items Providing Partial Credit" (DOI 10.1177/0013164421994636). | 🟢 |
+| 3.1 | Studies on multiple-response scoring generally find **partial-credit rules perform better psychometrically than all-or-nothing**, including Ripkey's rule and balanced rules; the differences are sometimes small. The app's rule (credit for correctly *unselected* options too) is a form of "balanced" scoring but gives a non-zero score to a blank answer — compare it with other rules. **Update 2026-11-19:** changed in the app to *correct picks minus wrong picks, floored at zero, blank = 0* (issue #1266). See [Psychometrika framework](https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/psychometrika/article/psychometric-model-framework-for-multiple-response-items/10D6DCD514D67281E4695FFC00C88B6A) and the 2021 EPM paper "Evaluating Different Scoring Methods for Multiple Response Items Providing Partial Credit" (DOI 10.1177/0013164421994636). | 🟢 |
